  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nBroad troughing continues developing over the Upper Great Plains\ninto the Great Lakes today while the Northeast remains on the \nwest side of large scale ridging over the western Atlantic. \nWhile height falls creep into western NY today, eastern NY and \nwestern New England remains under the influence of weak \nsubsidence, keeping us dry. Tonight, our upstream trough over \nthe Great Lakes amplifies and digs into the Ohio Valley as two \nshortwaves phase together with the parent cyclone taking on a \nnegative tilt. This results in strengthening upper level \ndiffluence over the mid-Atlantic as a warm front lifts northward\nwith a weak sfc low developing along the boundary. Meanwhile, \nstrengthening southwest flow aloft will also allow moisture to \nadvect into the Northeast with PWATs rising over 1.50\". Chances \nfor showers and even a slight chance for thunderstorms increases\nfrom southwest to northeast 06 - 12 UTC Sunday as the front \napproaches. Guidance continues to hint that the weak sfc low \nwill aid in a more organized area of rain/embedded storms. As \nmentioned in the previous discussion, the latest probabilistic \nguidance from the HREF continues to show potential for moderate \nto heavy rain late tonight into Sunday morning with a 50 - 75% \nchance for over 0.50\" of rain 09 to 15 UTC Sun and even a 25-50%\nchance for over 1\" of rain. However, our recent dry stretch \nwill likely limit flooding and WPC removed the marginal risk for\nexcessive rainfall.\n\nAfter this initial A.M period of rain/storms, we should have a \nbrief break before an occluded front and stronger height\nfalls from the closed parent low over the eastern Great lakes \narrive. There remain uncertainties regarding how much \ninstability can develop. While there will be plenty of low- \nlevel moisture, morning clouds may struggle to break for sun \nduring the small window before the occluded front arrives Sun \nP.M. Even still, mid-level dry air and cooler temperatures aloft\nwrap eastward as the parent cyclone matures, helping to steepen\nlapse rates with forecast soundings showing \"tall- skinny\" CAPE\ndeveloping. Given the high humidity, even limited insolation \nwill contribute to sufficient instability and help generate low-\ntopped convection. While 0-6km shear is rather impressive \nranging 40-50kts, 0-3km shear only ranges 20-30kts. Should weak \ninstability keep convection low-topped, storms will not tap into\nthe stronger deep layer shear, decreasing the severe weather \npotential. SPC expanded the marginal risk (level 1 of 5) for \nisolated severe weather to now cover much of eastern NY and \nwestern New England given persistent forcing ahead of the parent\ncyclone; however, weak instability/shear are listed as reasons \nwill severe weather may be limited. Main concerns are damaging \nwinds from any severe weather with a linear storm mode favored \nas shear vectors remain parallel to the boundary.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe vertically stack closed low and associated cold pool in the\neastern Great Lakes slowly advances eastward Monday into Tuesday\nsupporting slightly cooler than normal temperatures and \ncontinued moist cyclonic flow aloft. Coverage of showers and \neven low- topped convection increases Monday afternoon, \nespecially near/north of I-90, as additional shortwaves rotate \naround the base of the closed low. Given the cool pool aloft, it\nlikely will not take much upscale growth before lightning \noccurs with even small hail possible from any taller storms. As \nwinds aloft becoming westerly, a weak lake effect signal looks \nto develop downstream of Lake Ontario, supporting steadier \nperiods of rain directed into the western Adirondacks and Mohawk\nValley Monday P.M into Monday night. Unsettled conditions \ncontinue into Tuesday with additional diurnally driven showers \nagain mainly near/north of I-90 but shower coverage should be \nlower than Monday as the parent cyclone slowly exits into New \nEngland.",
